[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-02T09:46:32-07:00","id":1072061,"title":"","content":"They're closer to low-level conspiracy theories than Urban Legends, I'd say. The thing about &quot;fake news&quot; is that it tends to be things that are plausible, but are being dismissed out of hand- used as a term to discredit, rather than being outlandish by default.<br /> <br /> Urban Legends tend to have something of the unlikely about them. &quot;Russia meddling in the elections&quot; is a hop, skip and a jump away from &quot;Clown statue killed my family&quot; or even &quot;Babysitter on LSD cooked my baby.&quot;<br /> <br /> Then again it could be some sort of Rumourmonger Fae, weaving webs that draw the inquisitive down its rabbit hole; only those with the patience, the savvy or the madness to penetrate the miasma of fake news would stumble into the hedge, to be snapped up; eternal players in whatever new stories it decides to weave.Information is victory, knowledge is power, but built on false logic, the power simply drains away...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;The phrase 'flies on the heads of pins' appeared 43 times in that journal, sometimes in the middle of sentences or written in the margins.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4871-malkydel","name":"Malkydel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4871_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-02T21:03:27-07:00","id":1072349,"tit